    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** (76.28) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) anchor our outfield, representing our highest-ceiling plays. **James Wood** benefits from a massive 35% cumulative boost from park factors and a favorable matchup against Young (4.76 xERA), while **Mickey Moniak** is in a prime spot against Paredes, benefiting from a 19% SP skill advantage and a strong platoon edge. Meanwhile, **Josh Naylor** (66.96) remains an auto-start at 1B against Cecconi; his profile is bolstered by the right-handed matchup, providing a reliable floor that keeps him comfortably in the top tier of our daily rankings.\n\nOn the base-stealing front, **Jose Caballero** (66.07) is a priority start despite a mediocre matchup against Bennett. The optimizer heavily weights his speed profile, and the \"Slow SP Delivery\" tag at 100% stolen base probability adds a critical +2% to his score, making him a high-efficiency play for our roster\u2019s specific needs. Conversely, **Austin Riley** (46.71) sits near the marginal threshold due to a difficult draw against Webb, though the wind blowing out at the park provides a slight buffer. The most difficult bench decision involved **Cole Young** (44.73), who narrowly missed the cut. While he offers some value, the efficiency gains provided by the lower-order volatility of **Kyle Karros** and the specific park advantages afforded to our outfielders ultimately squeezed him out of the starting nine. \n\nRegarding risk management, note that **Dalton Rushing** (40.69) is hovering dangerously close to our \"below floor\" cutoff due to a L/L platoon disadvantage against Hart and a suboptimal #8 spot in the order. We are proceeding with him, but his usage is being monitored closely to preserve game caps. Additionally, while **Corbin Carroll** (60.73) remains a strong play, be aware that his score is suppressed by a tough draw against Sulser; however, his elite profile dictates he stays in the lineup unless weather developments suggest a total washout. We have elected to bench **Travis Bazzana**, **Alex Freeland**, and **Tyler Soderstrom** to prioritize the higher-ceiling outcomes of our active starters against weaker pitchers.",
